# Reverse Proxy and TLS

By default herold terminates HTTPS itself: the `kind = "public"` and
`kind = "admin"` listeners bind port 443 directly and serve the Suite
SPA, JMAP, and the admin UI over TLS. Many operators instead want a
reverse proxy (Caddy, nginx, Traefik) in front of the web UIs - to
serve other web apps on the same host, to put the webmail and admin
SPAs on separate hostnames, to add access controls, or simply to keep
certificate management in a familiar tool.

This chapter describes how to do that. It applies equally to a
native install and to the Docker image: the port contention it
resolves is the same either way.

## The problem: two ACME clients

A reverse proxy that obtains its own Let's Encrypt certificates and
herold both want the same scarce resources, and they collide in three
places:

- **Port 80** - herold's ACME HTTP-01 challenge listener binds `:80`;
  a proxy doing HTTP-01 wants `:80` too.
- **Port 443** - herold's public listener serves the SPA and JMAP on
  `:443`, and the TLS-ALPN-01 challenge also reuses `:443`; the proxy
  wants `:443` to terminate TLS.
- **Rate limits** - two ACME clients under the same registered domain
  share Let's Encrypt's per-domain issuance budget, and if they ever
  request a certificate for the *same* set of names they burn the
  duplicate-certificate limit.

The instinct is to disable herold's ACME client and let the proxy own
all certificates. That does not work, because **herold's ACME client
cannot be removed**. It does not just serve the web listeners - it
issues the certificates for:

- SMTP STARTTLS (25), submission (587), implicit TLS (465)
- IMAP (993 implicit, 143 STARTTLS) and ManageSieve
- the `mta-sts.<domain>` policy host
- DANE: herold's auto-DNS publishes the `TLSA` record from the MX
  certificate's key, so certificate rotation and the DNS record must
  stay coupled inside herold

A web reverse proxy cannot meaningfully own the STARTTLS certificates
for the mail protocols. So herold keeps an ACME client no matter
what; the task is only to stop it fighting the proxy.

## The solution: separate the two ACME clients onto different planes

Give the proxy ports 80 and 443 and the HTTP-based challenges; move
herold's ACME to the **DNS-01** challenge.

- The reverse proxy binds `:80` and `:443`, terminates TLS for the
  web hostnames (`webmail.example.com`, `admin.example.com`,
  `mta-sts.example.com`), and solves ACME with HTTP-01 / TLS-ALPN-01.
- herold's `public` and `admin` HTTP listeners become `tls = "none"`
  and bind `127.0.0.1`. The proxy upstreams to them.
- herold's `[acme]` block is set to `challenge_type = "dns-01"` with a
  DNS provider plugin. herold then needs **no inbound port** for ACME:
  it keeps issuing every mail-protocol and MTA-STS certificate, all
  validated through DNS.

The chicken-and-egg dissolves because the two ACME clients now live on
different planes - one in HTTP, one in DNS - and issue for disjoint
sets of hostnames, so neither port contention nor the
duplicate-certificate limit can bite. DANE stays entirely inside
herold: the MX certificate and its `TLSA` record rotate together,
untouched by the proxy.

```
                          :80  :443
   Internet  ----------->  proxy  --- ACME HTTP-01 ---> Let's Encrypt
                            |          (webmail / admin / mta-sts certs)
              127.0.0.1:8080|  (public listener, plaintext)
              127.0.0.1:9443|  (admin listener,  plaintext)
                            v
                          herold --- ACME DNS-01 ---> Let's Encrypt
   Internet --- 25/465/587/993/143 -->  (mail.example.com cert, via DNS plugin)
```

Use a webmail hostname **distinct from the MX name** -
`webmail.example.com`, not the `mail.example.com` you set as
`[server] hostname`. That is what keeps the proxy's certificate names
and herold's certificate names from overlapping.

## Step 1: herold configuration

A complete example is in
`docs/manual/user/examples/system.toml.reverse-proxy`. The changes
relative to a direct-HTTPS deployment are:

- Set `[server] public_base_url` to the externally-visible webmail
  URL, e.g. `https://webmail.example.com`, so generated links and the
  OIDC callback are correct behind the proxy.
- Set the `public` and `admin` listeners to `tls = "none"` and bind
  them to `127.0.0.1` (ports 8080 and 9443 in the example).
- Set `proxy_protocol = true` on those two listeners. herold then
  decodes the PROXY-protocol header the proxy prepends, so request
  logs, the audit log, and the rate limiters see the real client IP
  instead of `127.0.0.1`, and the request is treated as HTTPS so
  scheme-derived URLs (the OAuth `redirect_uri`) are correct. A
  `proxy_protocol = true` listener **rejects any connection that
  arrives without a PROXY header**, so enable it only once the proxy
  is configured to send one (Step 2).
- Leave `[server.ui] secure_cookies` at its default of `true`. The
  browser reaches the proxy over HTTPS, so the `Secure` flag is
  correct even though herold's own listener is plaintext on loopback.
- Keep the mail listeners (25/465/587/993/143) bound on the public
  interface. Omit `cert_file` / `key_file` on them: the certificate
  is served from the TLS store by SNI for `[server] hostname`,
  populated by the ACME client.
- Set `[acme] challenge_type = "dns-01"` and `dns_plugin = "..."`,
  and declare the matching `[[plugin]]` block. First-party DNS
  plugins: `herold-dns-cloudflare`, `herold-dns-route53`,
  `herold-dns-hetzner`, `herold-dns-manual`.

The pivotal precondition is the DNS-01 move: it requires your DNS to
be hosted somewhere herold has a plugin for, with an API token. If
that is not possible, see _Common operational issues_ below.

## Step 2: the reverse proxy

A worked Caddy configuration is in
`docs/manual/user/examples/Caddyfile.example`. Caddy is a good fit -
it obtains and renews certificates automatically and proxies
WebSocket and Server-Sent Events transparently - but nginx or Traefik
work equally well.

Whatever proxy you choose, three things must be right:

- **Proxy the whole public web surface, not just the SPA.** The Suite
  SPA talks to JMAP on its own origin, so the proxy's webmail vhost
  must also carry `/jmap`, `/.well-known/jmap`, the chat WebSocket at
  `/chat/ws`, the send API, and the Web Push endpoints. You cannot
  proxy only the static SPA files.
- **Pass WebSocket upgrades and SSE through.** `/chat/ws` needs the
  `Upgrade` handshake; JMAP push is `text/event-stream` and must not
  be buffered. Caddy does both automatically; nginx needs explicit
  `proxy_set_header Upgrade`/`Connection` and `proxy_buffering off`.
- **Generous timeouts.** The JMAP EventSource connection is
  long-lived; a short proxy read timeout will sever it repeatedly.
- **Send the PROXY-protocol header upstream**, to match the
  `proxy_protocol = true` listeners from Step 1. In Caddy this is the
  `proxy_protocol v2` transport option (see `Caddyfile.example`);
  nginx uses `proxy_protocol on` in the `stream` block or
  `proxy_set_header` is not enough -- it must be the real PROXY
  header. Without it, herold rejects every connection.

## Applying it: native and Docker

**Native install.** Replace `/etc/herold/system.toml` with the
reverse-proxy shape, set the DNS plugin's API token in the
environment herold runs under (the systemd unit, typically), install
and start the proxy, and `SIGHUP` herold to reload.

**Docker.** The image's baked-in config binds `0.0.0.0` inside the
container; the loopback restriction comes from how you publish ports.
For this topology, publish only the mail ports to the host and
publish the web ports to the host loopback for the proxy to reach:

```
docker run -d --name herold \
  -v "$(pwd)/herold-data:/var/lib/herold" \
  -v "$(pwd)/system.toml.reverse-proxy:/etc/herold/system.toml:ro" \
  -p 25:25 -p 465:465 -p 587:587 -p 993:993 -p 143:143 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:9443:9443 \
  ghcr.io/hanshuebner/herold:latest
```

The proxy then runs on the host (or its own container) and upstreams
to `127.0.0.1:8080` / `127.0.0.1:9443`. If the proxy also runs in a
container, put both on a shared Docker network and upstream to the
herold container's name instead of `127.0.0.1`.

## Common operational issues

### Every web request fails after enabling proxy_protocol

A listener with `proxy_protocol = true` requires the PROXY-protocol
header on every connection and rejects any connection without one.
The two ways to hit this:

- The proxy is not configured to send the header. Add the
  `proxy_protocol` transport option (Step 2).
- You opened the loopback port directly (`curl 127.0.0.1:8080`)
  rather than through the proxy -- a direct connection sends no
  header and is rejected. That is the intended anti-spoof behaviour;
  test through the proxy's hostname instead.

Enable `proxy_protocol = true` and the proxy's header together, never
one without the other.

### DNS-01 is not an option

If your DNS provider has no herold plugin and cannot be scripted, the
two ACME clients cannot be cleanly separated. The realistic fallback
is to make the proxy issue *all* certificates - including the
mail-protocol ones - and point herold's listeners at the resulting
files with `cert_file` / `key_file` (certificate source `file`),
disabling herold's ACME entirely. The cost is that DANE `TLSA`
rollover is then split across two systems and must be coordinated by
hand; do this only if you do not use DANE.

### Admin UI exposure

herold's `admin` listener is loopback-by-default deliberately.
Publishing it on a public hostname is a posture change. Restrict the
admin vhost with an IP allowlist at the proxy, or - stronger - leave
the admin listener reachable only over an SSH tunnel or VPN and proxy
only the webmail hostname.

